A||||9||Seanh||Ignition Module (Non-Fiero)||||5||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-14-2000||12:32 PM||||Sorrt for posting here but I am having some trouble on our '90 Town Car. It starts up and then usally dies. It wont die while your driving it, but when you slow down at a light or drive thru, it usally dies. It will start right back up again though. I am thinking it might be the ignition module? Sound right? I hate to spend the money for a new one and find out it wasnt the problem. I did the obvious thing, new plugs and wires, I also set the idle up a tad bit, but no luck. I pulled the fuel filter out and it was clogged, you couldnt even blow thru it. I got a compressor and blew it all out, and it was nasty. I replaced it and it seemed to run better for a while with alot better gas milage. Now it is starting to do it again. I havent check the fuel filter again yet, but I am thinking it might be somthing else. Is there any way to check the ignition module? Could it be my fuel pump maybe? Also, how do I check the fuel pump? I never replace the cap and rotor when I changed wires, possibly it? I have no idea. I was wondering what you guys think. Sound like a bad module or somthing else? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||5
Z||000001||862M4inCA||10-14-2000||03:06 PM||||This doesn't sound like the ignition module to me. In my experience when the module goes, it leaves you dead in the water until it's replaced. You CAN have it checked if your still uncertain tho. I know I had mine checked at the local Autozone for free. I would definately have the fuel filter replaced, especially if all you did was blow the old stuff out and put it back. Could be possible that using the compressed air created a hole somewhere in the filter rendering it virtually useless. I would also run some injector cleaner through, especially since you used the old filter again. I'm assuming that the check engine light has not come on, however it still might not be a bad idea to see if there are any stored codes. I'd also check for possible loose/broken vacuum hoses, definately check out the cap and rotor for carbon tracks, cracks and excessive corrosion. Hope this puts you in the right direction.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-14-2000||04:07 PM||||I did replace the old fuel filter. I only blew it out to see how clogged it really was. It was repled and the car ran good, but not perfect after that. I have ran countless fuel Injector cleaner thru the car. I will check for vacum leaks and the rotor and cap. I thought the same thing about the ignition module but a machanic told me to replace. I will check to see if I can get it checked here. All we have is a "Advance Auto Parts" store that I have had trouble with them times before. I will check with them though. No, check engine light does not come on. I recently had the alt. light come on and check engine light also. I replaced the voltage regulator, and fixed that problem. That is the only code on the computer present. Can you think of any sensors that might be bad that would cause this? It only happens at low or no speeds.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-14-2000||07:19 PM||||In Fiero if this was happening one of the things to check would be the Torque Converter lockup system. You might want to fing out how to disable that and see if this still happens. (I don't know the current crop of ford transmission. I know they use TC locks but don't know how it's controlled.)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mark||10-14-2000||07:20 PM||||Have you tried cleaning the throttle body and the idle air bypass path?  When you get off the throttle, it's up to the idle air control motor to maintain a steady idle speed.  If it's all gunked-up, the IAC motor response is sluggish, which results in stalling at idle.  Cleaning the idle air bypass path was the cure for stalling in my Dodge minivan.      ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||10-14-2000||07:26 PM||||I did try cleaning the throttle body. I never thought of the Idle air bypass. I will try that. Also, If that does not work, I will try the TC Lockup. Thanks Guys<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||10-14-2000||08:31 PM||||The lazy way to check for torque convertor lock would be put the tranny in neutral as you approach a stop, and see if it still dies. Does the engine die at idle too, or just when slowing to a stop? I would check owners manual to make sure putting it in neutral while moving won't damage the tranny. I don't want to be (inderectly) responsible for any damage that might occur.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pffsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||10-15-2000||08:30 PM||||It will die at any low RPS, at idle or at a stop. It seems to only do it some times, usally it will not do it once it warms up. I am thinking fuel delivery system for some reason? It will die in Net. also.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||10-15-2000||08:48 PM||||I would check the timing, engines can be more finicky when cold about timing. Does the engine still have normal power under acceleration, or does it seem to be lacking. I think it almost has to be something electrical.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||10-15-2000||08:54 PM||||Well, I cant tell you for sure about the power. It seems to have as much power as our 86 TownCar with the same size (5.0L SEFI) engine. However, (the trouble car is a 90) it has very little power compared to out 96 TownCar with a 4.6L SEFI and Distributor-less ingition system. (If you cant tell by now my family swears by TownCars) How do I check timing on this car? I have never did it before. I have a Hayne Manual for it, but cant for the life of me find it now. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
